# Runbook

Operator playbook. Linear, phase-by-phase, with concrete commands. Pair with the full methodology in [`README.md`](README.md).

> **Authorization required for everything in this document.** Run only against systems and accounts named in a signed RoE.

---

## Phase 0: Mobilization

- [ ] RoE signed; Control Group contacts saved offline
- [ ] Get-out-of-jail letter on operator phones (PDF + photo)
- [ ] Engagement Slack/Signal/Matrix room live; ops bridge tested
- [ ] Ghostwriter project created; everyone has access
- [ ] Infrastructure stood up (team server, redirectors, phishing infra, payload domains)
- [ ] Domains aged / categorized; mail records correct
- [ ] Beacon Malleable C2 profile written, reviewed, not default
- [ ] Loader chain built and tested against representative EDR
- [ ] Test phishing template sent to in-team mailbox; rendering and link-click verified
- [ ] Deconfliction process tested with Control Group

---

## Phase 1: Reconnaissance

### External
- [ ] Domains, subdomains, ASN, IP ranges enumerated
- [ ] Cloud tenant ID, M365 sign-in domains identified
- [ ] Edge tech fingerprinted (VPN, mail, identity providers)
- [ ] Employee enum (LinkedIn → email format → user list)
- [ ] Breach data reviewed for valid creds (within RoE)
- [ ] GitHub/GitLab org reviewed for leaks
- [ ] Cloud asset sweep (S3 / Blob / GCS / public DBs)
- [ ] Shodan / Censys queries saved

### Identity
- [ ] Email format confirmed and validated
- [ ] User list assembled (deduped, role-tagged where possible)
- [ ] MFA posture inferred (M365 endpoints, OWA responses)
- [ ] SSO provider identified (Okta, Entra, PingFederate, etc.)

---

## Phase 2: Initial Access

Pick a primary path and a fallback. Don't burn multiple paths on the first move.

### Phish — link (AiTM)
- [ ] Evilginx3 phishlet selected/customized for target SSO
- [ ] Domain warming complete
- [ ] Sending IP reputation checked
- [ ] Pretext written and reviewed
- [ ] Targeted recipient list approved with Control Group
- [ ] Send window agreed
- [ ] Click + capture verified end-to-end in test
- [ ] Token replay tested against M365/SSO

### Phish — attachment
- [ ] Delivery container chosen (HTML smuggling / ISO / LNK / etc.) based on target tech and MoTW behavior
- [ ] Loader verified executing in lab with target EDR profile
- [ ] Beacon callback verified through redirector chain
- [ ] Pretext + lure document ready
- [ ] Test send + receipt verified

### Password spray
- [ ] User list deduped against breach data
- [ ] Lockout policy understood (don't lock out users)
- [ ] Spray cadence: 1 password / N hours / per N users
- [ ] Tooling: MSOLSpray, TREVORspray, DomainPasswordSpray
- [ ] Conditional Access posture noted before spraying
- [ ] Hits validated immediately; tokens captured

### Public-facing exploit
- [ ] CVE(s) verified applicable to target version
- [ ] Exploit tested in lab against same version
- [ ] Foothold post-exploit plan ready (don't pop a shell with no plan)
- [ ] Control Group notified before exploitation against prod

### Foothold confirmation
- [ ] C2 callback received, sleep set conservatively (≥ 60s + jitter)
- [ ] Initial situational awareness from Beacon: `whoami /all`, `hostname`, `systeminfo` (or BOF equivalents — less noisy)
- [ ] AV/EDR identified (BOF: [edrenum-bof](https://github.com/EncodeGroup/edrenum-bof), Seatbelt `AntiVirus`)
- [ ] Logged callback in Ghostwriter

---

## Phase 3: Situational Awareness (post-foothold)

Quiet enumeration. Default tools are signatured — prefer BOFs and in-memory .NET.

- [ ] Local user/group context
- [ ] Local privileges (`whoami /priv`)
- [ ] Network position (`ipconfig /all`, route table, ARP cache)
- [ ] Domain joined? Domain controller reachable?
- [ ] Proxy / VPN posture for C2 egress validated
- [ ] AV/EDR product, version, definitions date
- [ ] Patch level / pending reboot
- [ ] Recent users (RDP history, profile dir)
- [ ] Mounted shares, mapped drives
- [ ] Saved credentials (DPAPI, browser, RDP)
- [ ] Installed software list (Seatbelt `Software`)
- [ ] Scheduled tasks, services, autoruns (Seatbelt `AllChecks`)

---

## Phase 4: AD Enumeration

If joined to AD, get a graph.

- [ ] SharpHound or SOAPHound run (collection methods chosen for OPSEC: DCOnly vs All)
- [ ] Output ingested to BloodHound CE
- [ ] Shortest paths to high-value targets identified
- [ ] Unconstrained delegation hosts mapped
- [ ] Kerberoastable accounts identified
- [ ] AS-REProastable accounts identified
- [ ] AD CS templates enumerated (Certipy `find -vulnerable`)
- [ ] LAPS attribute readable from current user? (check ACL)
- [ ] GPO write rights?
- [ ] DCSync rights anywhere on path?

---

## Phase 5: Credential Access

Pick the quietest viable technique first.

- [ ] Kerberoast where available; crack offline
- [ ] AS-REProast where available
- [ ] LSASS dump only if needed and EDR posture allows (nanodump preferred; consider PPL bypass requirements)
- [ ] DPAPI sweep on operator-controlled host
- [ ] Snaffler against accessible shares for cleartext creds
- [ ] SYSVOL review (`Groups.xml` cpassword in legacy environments)
- [ ] Browser credential extraction where in scope
- [ ] LAPS read where authorized

Crack offline on dedicated GPU rig, never on operator box.

---

## Phase 6: Privilege Escalation

### Local
- [ ] PrivescCheck or WinPEAS output reviewed
- [ ] SeImpersonate path (Potato variants) where applicable
- [ ] Service misconfig / unquoted path / writable PATH
- [ ] UAC bypass if needed for medium → high integrity

### Domain
- [ ] BloodHound path executed step by step
- [ ] Coercion + relay paths (PetitPotam / DFSCoerce → ntlmrelayx → LDAP / ADCS HTTP)
- [ ] Shadow Credentials on accounts with `GenericWrite` / `GenericAll`
- [ ] RBCD setup → S4U2self/S4U2proxy
- [ ] AD CS abuse (ESC1/ESC8 most common)
- [ ] DA achieved? Confirm separately via two independent methods before reporting

---

## Phase 7: Lateral Movement

- [ ] Target selected from objectives, not opportunism
- [ ] Quietest available auth method (Pass-the-Cert > Pass-the-Ticket > Pass-the-Hash > PSExec)
- [ ] Peer-to-peer SMB/TCP Beacon spawned on lateral host before disconnecting
- [ ] Egress reduced — one talker per segment
- [ ] Lateral movement logged in Ghostwriter with timestamp + source/dest

---

## Phase 8: Objective Execution

Per objective from RoE:

- [ ] Access path documented end-to-end
- [ ] Evidence captured (screenshots with redacted real data, file listings, sample documents per Control Group approval)
- [ ] Crown-jewel access demonstrated, not actioned
- [ ] If exfil authorized: encrypted archive, agreed channel, manifest of exact contents
- [ ] Control Group notified at each pre-agreed checkpoint
- [ ] Hard stops respected; pause and seek approval before crossing any

---

## Phase 9: Persistence (if engagement length warrants)

- [ ] Long-haul implant on separate infrastructure
- [ ] Persistence on host *not* used for active operations
- [ ] Persistence method documented for cleanup
- [ ] Trigger and dwell time tested
- [ ] OPSEC: long sleep, low-traffic, different malleable profile

---

## Phase 10: Cleanup

Every item created or modified must be removed or reverted.

- [ ] List all dropped files (BOFs, binaries, scripts) → delete
- [ ] List all created accounts → delete or disable per customer preference
- [ ] List all modified ACLs / group memberships → revert
- [ ] List all scheduled tasks / services / WMI subscriptions → remove
- [ ] List all registry persistence → remove
- [ ] List all certs issued (AD CS abuse) → notify customer for revocation
- [ ] List all forged tickets / Shadow Credentials → notify customer
- [ ] krbtgt reset recommended if Golden Ticket was used (× 2 with delay)
- [ ] All Beacon callbacks terminated
- [ ] Infrastructure decommissioned per schedule
- [ ] Operator hosts wiped

---

## Phase 11: Reporting

- [ ] Engagement timeline finalized
- [ ] Each finding mapped to ATT&CK technique IDs
- [ ] Reproduction steps verified by someone other than the executing operator
- [ ] Detection guidance written per finding (Sigma rules, log sources, sample queries)
- [ ] Executive summary written in business terms
- [ ] Attack path diagram exported
- [ ] IOC list compiled
- [ ] Draft reviewed internally
- [ ] Draft delivered for customer factual review
- [ ] Final report delivered

---

## Phase 12: Debrief

- [ ] Control Group debrief held
- [ ] Joint purple readout scheduled and held with SOC / blue
- [ ] Detection content delivered (rules, queries, log source recommendations)
- [ ] Customer sign-off on artifact removal
- [ ] Internal retrospective held; lessons logged
- [ ] TTP library / playbooks updated

---

## Emergency Procedures

- **Beacon caught / EDR alert observed** → don't task further, observe for response, notify Control Group on agreed channel within agreed window
- **Operator credentials suspected exposed** → rotate immediately, audit recent ops
- **Customer asks "is this you?"** → answer truthfully per RoE; the Control Group has the protocol for what to tell whom
- **Unintended impact (service degraded, account locked, data altered)** → stop, document, notify Control Group immediately

